sts_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"content_type":"application/json","state":"uploaded","size":20879,"digest":"sha256:f0d66ff295a22a2e2c31683dd78f840b49185cea9d1a1fa8e4d4cc8114f9564c","download_count":7064,"created_at":"2026-11-01T10:28:57Z","updated_at":"2026-11-01T10:28:58Z","browser_download_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/releases/download/v1.29.1/packslip.sigstore.json"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/releases/assets/698007682","id":698007682,"node_id":"RA_kwDOT-nLJc4pmsCC","name":"SHA256SUMS","label":"","uploader":{"login":"github-actions[bot]","id":41898282,"node_id":"MDM6Qm90NDE4OTgyODI=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/15368?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/github-actions","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"content_type":"application/octet-stream","state":"uploaded","size":705,"digest":"sha256:9aa4998a408fde7d9ce328557abc110b185ca77b9a289d1d6fe836866d34184c","download_count":65,"created_at":"2026-11-01T10:28:27Z","updated_at":"2026-11-01T10:28:27Z","browser_download_url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/releases/download/v1.29.1/SHA256SUMS"}],"tarball_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/tarball/v1.29.1","zipball_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mr-boxington/zipball/v1.29.1","body":"Fixes for build scripts that read environment variables with non-identifier names, `mbx gc` removing target directories that running programs use, Windows CI builds that could not find predictions from earlier lockfiles, grouped exports left empty after a workspace-state restore, and stale target directories after quick config edits.\n\n## Fixed\n\n- **Build scripts receive every environment variable** ([#888](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/888), @jdx). On systems where `/bin/sh` is dash (Ubuntu, Debian), build scripts lost variables whose names aren't valid shell identifiers, such as tauri's `DEP_TAURI_CORE:PATH__CORE_PLUGIN___PERMISSION_FILES_PATH`, and failed. Before, the only workaround was `MBX_BUILD_SCRIPT_EXECUTION=0`. The Unix build-script launcher now starts the pinned mbx binary directly through its shebang, with no shell in between. If the path contains whitespace or the shebang line would be longer than 127 bytes, mbx still uses the old `sh` launcher. Launchers installed by earlier versions keep working.\n- **`mbx gc` keeps target directories that running programs use** ([#886](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/886), @jdx). A program started directly from `target/`, such as a benchmark harness or an end-to-end test binary, could lose its target directory mid-run when a build in another checkout triggered collection over `target.max_size`. Budget and age collection now skip a target directory that one of your processes is using and report `kept N target directories in use by running commands`. Detection depends on the platform:\n  - **Linux:** a process that runs, maps or has open a file in the directory. This includes rust-analyzer when it has proc-macros loaded from that checkout.\n  - **macOS:** only the executable each process runs.\n  - **Windows:** a directory that Windows refuses to rename is kept.\n\n  The process scan is skipped if it takes more than 2 seconds or the process table can't be read. When the disk is under `gc.min_free_size`, collection works as before and can still remove these directories.\n- **Windows checkouts inherit predictions from earlier lockfiles** ([#870](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/870), @jdx). With Git's default `core.autocrlf=true`, which GitHub's Windows runners also use, the checked-out `Cargo.lock` has CRLF line endings but Git history stores LF. The two never matched, so a Windows build fell back to an unrelated manifest and could report `0 hits` with every prediction mismatched. The lockfile digest now treats CRLF as LF. Digests from earlier releases are still tried, so manifests already in a remote cache stay usable after the upgrade.\n- **Grouped exports keep crates restored from workspace state** ([#873](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/873), @jdx). After `mbx cache import` restored Cargo workspace state, a build compiled nothing, so a grouped export after cleaning the target (for example with the action's `clean-target-before-export: true`) could contain 0 actions, and the next job rebuilt everything. Grouped builds now include the actions from the restored bundle, except those for crates the build recompiled and those whose results are no longer in the store. Builds without an export group are unchanged.\n- **Same-length config edits no longer leave mbx using the old target directory** ([#881](https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/881), @lhzapata). Rewriting `.cargo/config.toml`, a `--config` file or `Cargo.toml` with content of the same length during the filesystem's timestamp tick (for example, changing `target-dir` from `…/aaa` to `…/bbb`) could leave mbx using the old Cargo metadata, including the old target directory. mbx no longer saves Cargo metadata read that soon after a file change, so the next build reads it again. This costs at most one extra `cargo metadata` call.\n\n**Full Changelog**: https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/compare/v1.29.0...v1.29.1","mentions_count":2}